course: dictionary
lesson: 6199
title: Misquote: when words are repeated wrongly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A quotation is someone's exact words, repeated inside quotation marks. To misquote is to repeat those words wrongly, so the new version is not what was really said. People might do it by mistake, or on purpose to change what the words mean.
explain: Look at the word itself. Mis- means wrong, as in misspell or misread, and quote means to repeat someone's words. So misquote means to repeat words wrongly. As a noun, a misquote is one wrong quotation.
explain: Many famous lines are misquoted. «Elementary, my dear Watson» never appears exactly like that in the original Sherlock Holmes books, yet people say it all the time. When you hear a famous line, check the original before you trust the exact words.
fact: Misquote means to repeat someone's words wrongly.
fact: As a noun, a misquote is a wrong quotation.
fact: Misquoting can be a mistake, or it can be done on purpose to change the meaning.
fact: «Beam me up, Scotty» was never said exactly like that in Star Trek.
fact: In the news, a misquote can spread false information, so always check the original source.
exercise: To misquote someone means to do what? | *Repeat their words wrongly | Repeat their words exactly | Write them a letter | Forget their name
exercise: «The ten percent myth spread through misquoted scientists, self-help books and films.» What does misquoted mean here? | Repeated word for word | *Repeated wrongly, so the words changed | Read out loud to a class | Painted in bright colours
exercise: Ella reads a famous line on a poster. She checks the original book and finds the words are different. What has happened to the line? | *It has been misquoted | It has been read aloud twice | It is a famous quotation | It has been translated into French
test: As a noun, what is a misquote? | A long speech | A type of book | *A wrong quotation | A famous actor
test: Why do news lessons teach you to check the original source? | To make the story longer | To learn a new language | To find the best photograph | *To catch a misquote that could spread false information
exam: Which line is a famous misquote? | A line from a song you sing in class | A sentence printed in a school textbook | *«Beam me up, Scotty», which was never said exactly like that in Star Trek | A headline from yesterday's newspaper
